course: dictionary
lesson: 13140
title: Forcibly
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Forcibly» is an adverb. It tells you how something was done. When something is done forcibly, someone uses force, so the person has no choice and does not want to do it. «The guard forcibly removed the noisy man from the hall.» The man did not want to leave, and the guard made him go.
explain: The word can also mean strongly and clearly. «She argued forcibly for a new slide in the park.» Here nobody is being pushed. She simply made her point very strongly, with plenty of energy and clear reasons.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Words like «removed», «taken» or «moved» often sit next to «forcibly» when someone is made to do something. Ask yourself: did the person want this to happen? If not, forcibly probably means by force.
fact: Forcibly means by using force, so that someone is made to do something against their will.
fact: «Forcibly removed» means someone was taken away from a place even though they did not want to go.
fact: Forcibly can also mean strongly and clearly, as in «He argued forcibly».
fact: Forcibly is an adverb, so it tells you how an action happened.
fact: Forcible is the adjective (as in «forcible entry»), and forcibly is its adverb form.
exercise: «The guard forcibly removed the noisy man from the hall.» What does forcibly mean here? | Very quietly | *By force, against his will | Politely, with a smile | Quickly, before lunch
exercise: «She argued forcibly for a new slide in the park.» What does forcibly mean here? | Secretly, behind a tree | *Strongly and clearly | Sadly and slowly | Without using any words
exercise: «The thief forcibly took the bag from the old lady.» Which word shows the lady had no choice? | thief | *forcibly | bag | old
test:
Ben says forcibly means gently. What does the word really mean? | *By force, against someone's will | Gently and kindly | Happily, with a song | Slowly, step by step
«The protesters were forcibly removed from the square.» What does this tell you? | They had no choice about leaving | *They had no choice about leaving | They left because they were tired | They danced out of the square
exam:
Which sentence uses forcibly to mean strongly and clearly? | «He ran forcibly across the field.» | *«He argued forcibly for a new school library.» | «He forcibly closed the door.» | «The bird forcibly flew away.»
